# Android Kotlin

Generate code, corrections, and refactorings that follow modern Kotlin and Android practice.

**Sources of truth (in order):**

1. `docs/architecture.md` — layers, packages, DI, integrations
2. `docs/prd/` — product behavior for the current delivery
3. [Android Architecture Recommendations](https://developer.android.com/topic/architecture/recommendations)
4. [Kotlin Coding Conventions](https://kotlinlang.org/docs/coding-conventions.html)

If project docs and a general habit conflict, **project docs win**.

## Kotlin

- Use English for all code and documentation comments.
- Prefer `val` and immutable data. Use `data class` for models.
- Declare types on public APIs (parameters, return types, exposed properties). Allow local type inference.
- Avoid `Any` when a concrete type or sealed hierarchy fits.
- Follow JetBrains naming: PascalCase for types and `.kt` files matching the primary type (`ProductViewModel.kt`); camelCase for functions and properties; `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` for `const val`; packages lowercase without underscores.
- Prefer composition over inheritance. Declare interfaces for contracts. Keep classes small and single-purpose.
- Keep functions short and single-purpose. Name with a verb. Booleans: `isX`, `hasX`, `canX`. Prefer early returns over deep nesting.
- Prefer few typed parameters; use a `data class` only when the grouping is real, not as a default RO-RO pattern.
- Prefer `val` over mutable state. Avoid blank lines inside a function body.
- Use exceptions for unexpected failures. For expected domain/API outcomes (not found, validation, network), prefer typed results or sealed outcomes over throwing. Catch to recover or add context; otherwise let a higher handler deal with it.

## Android architecture (Google-aligned)

- Single module `:app` and single-Activity (`MainActivity`) unless `docs/architecture.md` says otherwise.
- **UI layer:** Jetpack Compose + Material 3. Do not use XML layouts, Fragments, ViewBinding, or ConstraintLayout for new screens.
- **Navigation:** Jetpack **Navigation 3** (`NavDisplay` / back stack / type-safe keys). Do not introduce Navigation Compose (Nav2) in new code.
- **Data layer:** repositories are the only entry point. ViewModels must not talk to Retrofit, Room DAOs, DataStore, or device APIs directly.
- **Domain layer:** optional. Do **not** add use-case / domain packages by default. Introduce them only when logic is reused across ViewModels or a ViewModel becomes too complex — per Google guidance for larger apps. If present, keep domain pure JVM (no Android, Retrofit, or Room).
- **UDF / MVVM:** UI calls ViewModel methods; ViewModel exposes immutable `StateFlow` UI state (prefer a single `uiState`, or sealed `*UiState` when states are exclusive). Do not use MVI reducers/intents as the default pattern. Do not emit one-shot “events” from ViewModel to UI — handle the action and update `uiState`.
- Collect with `collectAsStateWithLifecycle`. Prefer `StateFlow` over LiveData for new Compose code. Use `stateIn(..., SharingStarted.WhileSubscribed(5_000), ...)` when bridging repository Flows.
- ViewModels at **screen / destination** level only. Reusable composables use plain state holders + hoisting, not ViewModels. Use `ViewModel`, not `AndroidViewModel`. Keep ViewModels free of `Activity`, `Context`, and `Resources`.
- Async work in `viewModelScope` with Coroutines and Flow.
- DI: Hilt (`@HiltAndroidApp`, `@HiltViewModel`, constructor injection).

## Package layout

Organize by **feature/function**, with layer subpackages when useful (see `docs/architecture.md`):

```text
core/di/
core/network/
core/database/
core/ui/theme/
featureOrArea/
  data/          # repository, data sources, mappers, models as needed
  ui/            # screens, screen ViewModels, UiState
navigation/
```

Do not invent a global mandatory `domain/` tree. Shared UI pieces live under a clear shared package (e.g. `core/ui` or `ui/components`) only when reused.

## Networking, storage, on-device

- Remote: Retrofit + OkHttp + kotlinx.serialization (or the stack fixed in `docs/architecture.md`).
- Honor API/client policies from architecture (e.g. required User-Agent).
- Local persistence: Room (or DataStore when the architecture specifies) behind repositories.
- Map DTOs/entities to app models in the data layer before the UI needs them.
- On-device ML/camera (e.g. CameraX, ML Kit) stays on device; do not upload frames/images unless the PRD explicitly requires it.
- Do not add Coil/image loading, auth, or analytics unless product docs include them.

## UI composition pattern

```kotlin
// Good: screen ViewModel + StateFlow + lifecycle-aware collect
@HiltViewModel
class ProductViewModel @Inject constructor(
    private val productRepository: ProductRepository,
) : ViewModel() {
    private val _uiState = MutableStateFlow<ProductUiState>(ProductUiState.Idle)
    val uiState: StateFlow<ProductUiState> = _uiState.asStateFlow()

    fun lookup(code: String): Unit {
        viewModelScope.launch {
            _uiState.value = ProductUiState.Loading
            _uiState.value = productRepository.lookup(code).toUiState()
        }
    }
}

@Composable
fun ProductRoute(viewModel: ProductViewModel = hiltViewModel()) {
    val uiState by viewModel.uiState.collectAsStateWithLifecycle()
    ProductScreen(uiState = uiState, onLookup = viewModel::lookup)
}

// Bad: MVI boilerplate, XML/ViewBinding, LiveData for new Compose,
// ViewModel calling Retrofit/DAO, or Navigation Compose (Nav2) for new screens
```

## Testing

- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert. Name fixtures clearly: `inputX`, `fakeX` / `mockX`, `actualX`, `expectedX`.
- JVM unit tests for mappers/repositories and screen ViewModels (JUnit, Turbine, kotlinx-coroutines-test).
- Prefer **fakes** over mocks (Google testing guidance). Use MockK when a fake is impractical.
- Do not require Robolectric for pure ViewModel/mapper tests. Do not use Flutter widget testing conventions.
